hc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/01-pull-and-status.md:17:- Status read, full shape: tail tokens vs threshold, compact recommendation, derivation counts by state (pending/retrying/failed/blocked), view health `null` pre-compact, visibility zone sum vs max\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/01-pull-and-status.md:33:- **AC-1.7**: Two pulls with no intervening intake, boundary advance, or compact return byte-identical output.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/01-pull-and-status.md:34:- **AC-2.8**: The status read returns: tail size against the configured trigger threshold and a compact recommendation; derivation counts by state (pending, retrying, failed, blocked); the active view's degraded-entry and gap counts; and the visibility zone's token sum against its max — reads only, callable any time, no side effects.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/01-pull-and-status.md:55:| `src/domains/thread-view/internal/boundary.ts` | position read only (advance is Story 4) |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/01-pull-and-status.md:84:`status` computes the visibility zone sum with the same deleted-filtered indexed SUM the Story 4 advance will use — build it once in `boundary.ts` now, parameterized by position, so Story 4 consumes it rather than re-deriving it (the design requires the two sums equal; sharing the query makes that structural). `compactRecommended` is `tailTokens > threshold`, nothing smarter — the caller owns policy. Derivation counts come from the owners' report surfaces, not direct `derived_form` reads (must-not-own).\npackages/lhc/test/derivation-messages.test.ts:179:    const summary = formOf(filePath, \"m3\", \"tool_result_summary\");\npackages/lhc/test/derivation-messages.test.ts:335:      workItemId: \"w-m2-tool_result_summary-v1\",\npackages/lhc/test/derivation-messages.test.ts:337:      kind: \"tool_result_summary\",\npackages/lhc/test/derivation-messages.test.ts:353:      [\"w-m2-tool_result_summary-v1\", \"done\"],\npackages/lhc/test/derivation-messages.test.ts:357:    // One summary form, outcome repaired, source version advanced — the\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/05-render-targets.md:18:- Process-suite parity legs for all five commands (closes the claimed-vs-proven CLI gap), the profile-violation exit-nonzero leg, and the CLI-intake-advances-boundary architecture-risk leg (Story 4's named debt)\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/05-render-targets.md:43:The capstone: the view crosses two boundaries — file format and process — and must arrive unchanged at both. `materialize.ts` never reads the record: it renders `pull`'s output, which makes AC-5.3's parity structural rather than tested-into-existence. `cli/view.ts` is the last surface piece, and the process suite here is where three other stories' deferred legs land (Story 3's TC-3.3 CLI leg, Story 4's CLI-advance leg, the all-five parity rows).\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/05-render-targets.md:66:| Process-suite legs | test-plan.md L64–65 (CLI parity + CLI-advance rows), L47 |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/05-render-targets.md:85:| CLI-advance leg | Story 4 | spawned CLI intake over max → pull shows flips, status zone ≤ target (closes Story 4 debt) |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/05-render-targets.md:90:The two process-suite rows above are this story's architecture-risk load: claimed-vs-proven CLI surface, and the seam-install class (a createSdk-only advance install passes every in-process test and fails the spawned leg). Zero-provider now asserted on CLI paths too — view commands run with **no provider configured at all** (the design's prerequisite line), which is itself the proof that no view operation needs one.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/05-render-targets.md:116:- [ ] CLI-intake-advances-boundary leg green (closes Story 4's debt comment)\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/03-readiness-sweep.md:86:Buckets come from the *owners' report joins*, not raw form states: \"retrying\" is a report-level distinction (pending + queue detail), and the sweep must not re-derive it from `derived_form` reads. The receipt's `requeued` carries subject ids so TC-3.4 can follow specific forms through heal. The classification table starts minimal (the design's named classes); unknown codes landing in `permanentFailed` with their literal reason is the receipt's visibility mechanism — expanding the table is config-tier work later, not a redesign.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/00-foundation.md:5:Migration v6 (view, band, boundary tables), profile config parsing and validation, the advance-seam injection point, and the derived-thread fixture every later story compacts against.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/00-foundation.md:15:- Migration v6: `thread_view` (singleton row, CHECK-enforced), `thread_view_band`, `view_boundary` (singleton, seeded to position 0) — per tech design §Storage\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/00-foundation.md:17:- The test injection facility — one mechanism, two named points: the post-commit advance (TC-4.6's failure injection) and compact's write path between sweep and view write (TC-2.4's crash injection); production code carries the points as no-ops unless a test installs a hook\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/00-foundation.md:30:- **FC-0.1**: Migration v6 applies cleanly to an Epic-02 thread file; all three tables exist with their CHECK constraints; `view_boundary` is seeded with position 0; re-applying is a no-op.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/00-foundation.md:35:- **FC-0.6**: The injection facility exists with both named points — post-commit advance and compact write path — reachable from tests without touching production code paths; uninstalled, both points are no-ops.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/00-foundation.md:52:| `src/shared/migrations.ts` (existing chain) | v6: `thread_view`, `thread_view_band`, `view_boundary` + seed boundary row at position 0 |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/stories/00-foundation.md:56:| test injection facility | two named points: post-commit advance, compact write path; no-op unless a test installs a hook; lives with the seam utilities, pattern from Epic 02's crash-injection seam |\npackages/lhc/test/goldens/README.md:28:| `boundary-g1-trajectory.json` | Boundary G1 (Story 4) | advance trajectory: over-max trigger, oldest-first flips to target, whole-message floor join, oversized-newest protected alone, never-backward across batches |\npackages/lhc/test/goldens/boundary-g1-trajectory.json:27:      \"label\": \"B4 under max after the advance: zone 70 <= 100, no movement (never backward, no churn)\",\npackages/lhc/test/cli-process-work.test.ts:181:      [\"w-m3-tool_result_summary-v1\", \"queued\"],\npackages/lhc/test/cli-process-work.test.ts:204:      [\"w-m3-tool_result_summary-v1\", \"done\"],\npackages/lhc/test/view-compact.test.ts:100:      boundary: db.prepare(`SELECT position FROM view_boundary`).all(),\npackages/lhc/test/view-compact.test.ts:123:    const row = db.prepare(`SELECT position FROM view_boundary`).get() as {\npackages/lhc/test/view-compact.test.ts:205:      (line) => line.owner === \"messages\" && line.kind === \"tool_result_summary\",\npackages/lhc/test/view-compact.test.ts:659:    // The suite fixture carries two scripted FAILED tool_result_summary forms\npackages/lhc/test/view-compact.test.ts:779:    // Next-compact visibility: the record changed under the snapshot — the\npackages/lhc/src/domains/turns/internal/compose.ts:120:    form: \"tool_result_summary\",\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:11:Epics 01 and 02 built a thread record that knows things and a derivation pipeline that summarizes them, and neither produces anything a model can be prompted with. The record holds events, messages, turns, chunks; the pipeline lands seven kinds of derived form beside them. What a harness needs is one assembled artifact: the conversation, arranged as a fidelity gradient, sized to a budget, in the shape its provider API takes. This epic builds that artifact — the thread view — and the four operations around it: pull, compact, sweep, and the visibility boundary that keeps the live tail from drowning in tool output.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:13:Two cost structures dominate the design, and they pull in the same direction: do nothing per-turn. The pull sits on the hot path between the user's action and the model call, so it must be reads and string assembly — every judgment (which chunk in which band, which tool result short) was made earlier by an explicit operation. And the harness caches its prompt prefix; every byte that changes above the newest content is money. The MVP's tool-result truncation re-decided every result's visibility on every message — the prefix churned every turn, which at Opus-class pricing is roughly $0.15 per turn, forever. The replacement design changes the prompt only at discrete, attributable events: a compact (explicit, rare), or a boundary advance (batched, budget-triggered). Between those events, identical pulls return identical bytes.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:21:Epic 03 validated clean for design: every AC maps to implementation work, contracts are complete, edge TCs exist (corruption refusal, advance failure, monster turn, never-compacted thread). Design-time entries:\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:26:| Boundary advance in manual mode | AC-4.9 (\"automatic\") | The advance runs in **both** host modes: it registers on `ctx.onCommit` and executes synchronously at flush. Unlike the drain (inference, background-only), the advance is cheap and deterministic — a CLI intake must advance the boundary too, or CLI-driven threads bloat. \"Automatic\" means SDK-internal, not background-mode-only. | Resolved — clarified |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:42:    TV -->|owns| VS[(thread_view + view_boundary tables)]\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:43:    IN[intake-stream] -.->|onCommit: advance check| TV\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:89:| `intake-stream` | Inherited | One additive touch: registers the advance check on `ctx.onCommit` (mirror of Epic 02's poke registration) |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:98:    intake-stream/index.ts        ← gains: one onCommit registration (advance check), beside the existing poke\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:105:        boundary.ts               ← NEW: visibility boundary row IO; advance check; compact reset\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:115:Boundary rules, one addition: `thread-view` imports `messages`/`turns` through their `index.ts` surfaces only, never internals; nothing imports `thread-view/internal`; **and `intake-stream` imports the `thread-view` surface** (the advance registration, Flow 4) — sanctioned by the surface-call rule, named here so Story 0's boundary gate expects it. The check-boundaries script gains both lines: thread-view's allowed imports, and intake-stream's added thread-view-surface line. Note for the script: this creates the domain graph's first surface-level cycle (intake → thread-view → messages ← intake) — runtime-safe (registration-then-flush, no import-time execution), but if the script models a DAG it needs a sanctioned-cycle annotation, not a silent pass.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:125:| `internal/boundary.ts` | NEW | Boundary row, advance check (sum → move → write), reset | sqlite, config | AC-4.1–4.9 |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:159:CREATE TABLE view_boundary (\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:166:One active view: `thread_view` holds at most one row; compact's transaction deletes and inserts (AC-2.6 — the cascade clears bands with it). The full band is not stored — it *is* the record after `compact_point`, read live at pull. `view_boundary` is a singleton row seeded at migration to position 0 (everything full). Budgets live in config, not rows.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:187:### Boundary advance (`boundary.ts`)\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:198:| Tail tool result (short) | `tool_result_summary` | deterministic truncation (existing Epic 01 abbreviation rule) | — | n/a (raw content always exists) |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:213:| `tool_result` (at-or-behind boundary) | `user` | `[tool result · <name> · abridged]\\n<summary or deterministic truncation>` (per the short-form ladder) |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:219:user:      [tool result · read_file · abridged]\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:221:           coalescing; poke registration on commit. [full content in record §m214]\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:287:**Covers:** AC-4.1–4.9. Two writers only: the post-commit advance (described above) and compact's reset.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:289:**Wiring (DD — the advance seam, pinned).** Intake-stream imports the thread-view *surface* (sanctioned: domains call each other's surfaces in-process, never internals — the same rule that lets intake call `messages.createFromEvent`). At the end of batch projection it registers the advance on `ctx.onCommit`: advance first (synchronous, cheap), queue poke second (fires async drain); the advance call is wrapped so a throw is caught and diagnosed, never eats the poke, never reaches intake's caller (isolation half of epic TD-Q1). The advance runs at flush in **both host modes** — unlike the poke it is not mode-gated, because it is deterministic and cheap; this is what keeps CLI-driven threads from bloating. **Budgets resolve like the poke resolves** (epic-fix-001's instance seam): an SDK operation carries its instance's `SdkViewConfig.visibility` on the per-instance seam; below-SDK direct domain calls fall back to the defaults (32000/24000/8000). The CLI builds a real SDK (`cli/work.ts` pattern), so CLI intake gets config-or-defaults through the same path — no separate CLI budget channel, no silently different behavior. The advance's `SUM(token_estimate)` filters deleted messages — the sum must equal what pull would render.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:297:`src/shared/view.ts` — the vocabulary (abridged to the load-bearing shapes; field comments carry AC refs in source):\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:323:  visibility: { zoneTokens: number; maxTokens: number };\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md:352:Stubs are structured-result: each returns `{ ok: false, errorClass: \"system_error\", reason: \"not implemented: <op>\" }` until Green (machine-readable contract; no throw stubs on this surface). Internal pure funct